{"annotationDigest":"sha256:c50102fe6379456a2081e0fbcf7429de0ff5ba9c953bed18960b3e3b773fcca4","annotationId":"rann-4d514f78a7fff9b0b3840b8ada260dc1","author":{"agent":"claude-code","kind":"ai","model":"claude-opus-5.5"},"createdAt":"2026-10-03T08:11:00Z","disclaimer":"AI-generated review opinion derived from the evidence package. It is not a verdict, does not change readiness, and must be confirmed by a human reviewer.","disclosureCheck":{"blocking":0,"contradictions":[],"disclosures":3,"nextDecision":"human_judgement","readiness":"ready"},"findings":[{"detail":"`test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh` has no functions; it runs 8 top-level rows against the real script with a stub selector. Its line 5 `# Verifies: SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ, SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y` (added by our proof work in 03b31789) gives it a file-level trace. review.scope still flags the unit `behaviour_without_intent` / `weak_mapping`, but the package raises no spec-gap candidate for it (`specGapCandidates` is empty). The rows match the two requirements: symlink root offered, selected and format-filtered; link and real roots merged; interior loop keeps exit 0; broken link root rejected with Path not found; link-plus-target and literal duplicate roots give one row. The menu-shell suite runs it as its own JUnit testcase (proof.yaml:201 catch-all loop), and review.tests records it as passed. A function-level trace cannot exist for a script with no functions.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file","line":5},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file","line":201}],"severity":"info","title":"The PR's test is traced at file level to the requirements its rows assert","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"Authored by our proof work (03b31789, wording fixes f161fcd2/93cd153f), not upstream. It is `observed_pass` (3 passed) and its inherited error_handling cell is `observed_pass` (1 test). Witnesses in `menu-file-test.sh`: a link root with a loop below it lists `clip.webm` and exits 0 (line 88); a real root lists as before (line 96); a link root whose target is gone is refused through `refuse()` (line 137). On the base, find skips a link start point, so the first row would list nothing. The defensive violation row is reviewed in REVIEW-261002-6P46. Status is `review` / `in_review` (ai_generated), so it is not yet human-approved spec.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh","kind":"file","line":88},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54}],"severity":"medium","title":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ (added): symbolic link roots are followed, links inside a root are not","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ is in review.changes (added, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"Authored by our proof work (03b31789). It is `observed_pass` (3 passed) and its error_handling cell is `observed_pass`. Witnesses: a link root plus its target (line 105), and a root given twice, each give one `clip.webm` row; a missing path next to a real root is refused with no partial listing (line 141). REVIEW-261002-VZ1K covers the defensive row. The rationale says the key is the file, not the path, so hard links also merge, and states that the PR gives the intent for aliased roots only. Status `review` / `in_review`.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh","kind":"file","line":105},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"severity":"medium","title":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y (added): a file reached through two given paths is listed once, keyed on device:inode","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y is in review.changes (added, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"The script changed under an unchanged HR29; the trace re-review restamped it (2c4063db, our proof work; `review.traceReviewRestamped`). `observed_pass` (2 passed). Its error_handling cell (inherited from SYS-REQ-260922-6642) is `observed_pass` via the `refuse()` rows (menu-file-test.sh:131). Its own external_call_timeout_bounded cell is `not_linked`; review.changes lists the class under `acceptedKiDebt`, tracked by KI-MENU-SELECT-POLL-DEADLOCK (HR29 spec line 76), which pre-dates this PR.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh","kind":"file","line":131},{"id":"specs/software/requirements/SW-REQ-260922-HR29.req.yaml","kind":"file","line":76}],"severity":"info","title":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29 (code_changed, preserved): formats, dotfiles, mtime order, missing-path rejection","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29 is in review.changes (code_changed, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$root\" \"jpg\" where $root/a/x.jpg and $root/b/x.jpg are hard links to one inode: base offers both paths, head offers one"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"`awk -F'\\t' '!seen[$1]++'` on `%D:%i` keys on the file, not the root alias. Two distinct paths that are hard links to one inode (e.g. a photo hard-linked into two album folders) were two rows at base. At head they are one row, and the other path cannot be picked. Re-run locally with bfs and a stub selector: base offers `r/a/x.jpg` and `r/b/x.jpg`, head offers only `r/a/x.jpg`. Codified as the code behaves: 9H5Y's rationale and hazard review (edge_case) and REVIEW-261002-VZ1K state it. Still only a question: the PR states the intent for aliased roots only, no upstream text says hiding a hard-linked path is wanted, and no test runs a hard link. The device:inode key is the fix our earlier GitHub review on this PR suggested; the PR adopted it at 03e6af6a. Upstream's later commit cc710e11 (not evaluated here) adds the file name to the key for this reason.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: is it intended that hard-linked files collapse to one picker row?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Hard-linked files under one root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file","bin/omarchy-transcode"],"scenario":"omarchy-transcode with ~/Videos a symlink to a tree containing a mode-000 subdirectory: the user picks a file, and omarchy-menu-file exits 1 under pipefail so omarchy-transcode aborts"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Under `set -euo pipefail` the pipeline status is the rightmost non-zero one. `find -H ... 2>/dev/null` exits 1 on Permission denied but still prints the readable rows. The selector returns the pick, the script exits 1, and `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` (`input=$(omarchy-menu-file ...)` under `set -e`) aborts after the pick. This already happens for a real-directory root at base. -H makes it reachable through link roots (e.g. `~/Videos -> /mnt/disk` with a root-owned `lost+found`), which listed nothing at base. Re-run locally with bfs: a link root to a tree with a mode-000 directory lists nothing and exits 0 at base; at head the stub selector gets the pick and the script exits 1. Not codified as a guarantee: FFTQ's hazard review and REVIEW-261002-6P46 name it explicitly as open and not covered by the requirement. The PR's interior-loop row covers loops, not permissions.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: can a pick succeed and the script still exit 1 when a followed symlink root contains an unreadable directory?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"two files with identical mtime under one root, and aliased roots Pictures->Videos: compare row order and the surviving path spelling at base and head"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Base `sort -rn` reverses the whole-line tiebreak, so equal-mtime rows were ordered by path descending. Head `sort -t$'\\t' -k2,2rn` makes `r` apply to the key only, so ties fall back to an ascending whole-line comparison that starts with `D:i`. Re-run locally on base and head with a stub selector and bfs (a GNU-compatible find, not GNU find): two equal-mtime files list b, a at base and a, b at head; roots `Videos:Pictures` (Pictures a link to Videos) keep `Pictures/c.jpg` at head in either argument order, where base listed only `Videos/c.jpg`. Partly codified: REVIEW-261002-VZ1K says the lexically smaller line is kept, and 9H5Y's hazard review says the spelling depends on the sort order of equal rows. HR29 states mtime-descending only. No test pins the spelling the picker returns or the tie order.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: which alias spelling survives the dedup, and is the new equal-mtime order wanted?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$tmp/link.jpg\" \"jpg\" where link.jpg is a symlink to a real jpg: base offers no row, head offers one"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"`-H` dereferences every start point. A root that is a link to a regular file with a matching extension was `-type l` at base (not listed). At head it is `-type f` and listed as one row. `[[ -e $path ]]` accepts it either way. Re-run locally with bfs: base offers no row, head offers `flink.jpg`. Codified as the code behaves in FFTQ's hazard review (edge_case). FFTQ's description still speaks of links to directories only. The PR describes directory roots only. No test covers it.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":32},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed now?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Symlink-to-file root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"detail":"review.compat is `compatible`: test_diff, mcdc_diff (85